"""Original Cyber Explorers Discover manuscript; external reference review pending."""
COURSE = {
  "key": "explorers-discover-v1",
  "title": "Everyday Digital Risks & Safety Choices",
  "academy_id": "secondary",
  "stage": "discover",
  "pathway": "secondary",
  "description": "Cyber Explorers · Stage 1. Explore how ordinary online decisions affect people, accounts, privacy and trust. Eight reading-led lessons with original examples and reflections.",
  "revision": "v1",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "position": 1,
      "title": "Recognising the moment of risk",
      "content": "Digital risk begins with a decision, not necessarily a dramatic attack. A message that asks you to log in, an app requesting permission or a friend forwarding a rumour creates a choice about trust. Ask what the request wants, which information or service is at stake, and what would happen if it were false. A familiar logo, confident language or a high number of likes is not evidence. Different actions have different consequences: reading a message does not expose a password, but entering a password on an unfamiliar page may. The first skill is separating what you observed from what you inferred.\n\nWorked example: Tola sees a notice saying her school account expires today. She does not assume the message is genuine or fraudulent from its appearance alone. She opens the school portal using a previously trusted bookmark and checks the notice there. She can now decide based on evidence from the real service, not the urgency of the message.\n\nPractise: Write two columns titled Observation and Assumption for a fictional account-warning message. List the sender name and deadline under observations; put claims such as 'the school sent it' under assumptions. What independent source would settle the question?\n\nStart with a decision map. In the first column write the event: a new sign-in alert, a link to a school document or a request for location access. In the second column write what you actually know from direct observation. In the third column write what someone wants you to infer. Finally list a low-risk action that could help verify the request. The point is to develop habits that apply beyond obvious scams. A message might be authentic and still use unnecessary pressure; an unfamiliar request might be legitimate but require independent checking. What matters is aligning the strength of evidence with the possible cost of the choice.\n\nExamine consequences before acting. Giving an application access to your exact location has different effects from simply reading a website's public article. Entering an account password into a page found through a link has different effects from seeing an unfamiliar logo. Try ordering fictional activities from lower to higher impact and explaining each ranking. There may not be one universally correct order because the information involved, the service and the learner's circumstances matter. Good safety education helps people reason rather than fear every unfamiliar interface.\n\nDiscuss the difference between safe delay and unnecessary delay. A genuine urgent safety notice may need prompt action. You do not need to spend an hour investigating a fire alarm; instead, follow the established safety procedure. By contrast, a message demanding a password within ten minutes creates time pressure to bypass ordinary verification. Distinguishing these situations requires knowing who is responsible for the relevant service and what an approved communication looks like. Prepare trusted contacts before an emergency so verification does not depend on the sender of the disputed request.\n\nPractice: write a fictional notice that sounds convincing but provides weak evidence of identity. Then rewrite it as a legitimate service notice that does not request private credentials and includes an independently reachable support channel. Explain what changed. A well-designed message can reduce the recipient's uncertainty, but even a polished message is not conclusive evidence. Finish by describing two cases where seeking help is the best decision. Help may come from a teacher, family member, service provider or responsible support team; it should never require handing over private account secrets.\n\nTo test your decision map, ask a peer to supply a second interpretation of the same observations. Compare what each interpretation would require you to do. For example, a sign-in alert might reflect your own recent device change or an unauthorised attempt. The appropriate check is the real account's recent activity, not accepting either explanation based on instinct. Record the outcome and revise your conclusion. This exercise develops a habit of considering alternatives without becoming paralysed by them."
    },
    {
      "position": 2,
      "title": "Checking identity without revealing secrets",
      "content": "Verifying an identity means using a route that is independent of the request you are checking. A phone number inside a suspicious message may connect you to the person who wrote it. Instead, use a trusted contact already saved, the official app or a website address you have independently verified. An unexpected request for a password, one-time code or authentication approval is a reason to stop. Genuine support teams do not need you to disclose those secrets in a chat. Even a message from a familiar account may be sent after that account is misused.\n\nWorked example: A relative appears to request emergency transport money from a new number. Chidi calls the relative through an existing contact method and discovers that the request was not genuine. The key control was the independent conversation, not whether the message sounded convincing.\n\nPractise: Compare three ways of verifying a message: replying to it, calling the number inside it and opening the service independently. Explain which method shares the least trust with the original claim.\n\nIdentity verification is about channels as well as names. A display name can be copied, a social account can be misused and a familiar photo can appear in an impersonation attempt. Evidence becomes stronger when you contact the person through a route established before the disputed message arrived. If a service asks for an unusual action, open the official app or a known address rather than trusting the message's own link and telephone number. This is an example of independent verification: the route used to check a claim is not controlled by the same source making the claim.\n\nThink about who is authorised to ask for what. A genuine support worker might need an error code or a brief description of a problem, but should not ask a learner to read out a password or one-time authentication code. A teacher may need to confirm attendance without needing students' account passwords. A friend may need help paying for transport but does not need control over the recipient's digital wallet. These boundaries are easier to apply when you understand the function of the information being requested instead of relying solely on whether the requester appears trustworthy.\n\nWorked decision: you receive a chat claiming that a friend's email account has been locked. The sender asks you to receive a security code on your own phone and forward it to them. Consider two possibilities: the friend genuinely needs help, or a fraudster is trying to register or access a service using your phone number. Both possibilities call for independent contact with the friend. Even if the person is genuinely your friend, forwarding a code you did not request for your own account may compromise your own access. The safer alternative is to help the friend locate the official recovery instructions for their service.\n\nPractice communicating the boundary respectfully. You might say that you want to help, but will not share a verification code or password because those are meant to authorise actions in your account. You can offer to call the person's usual number, look up the service's published recovery guidance or accompany them while they contact support. This protects the relationship rather than treating the person as an enemy. Reflection: how could the same rule apply to a school administrator, employer or bank employee who contacts you unexpectedly?\n\nOne final distinction is between confirming the person and confirming the action. A trusted friend might truly be speaking to you but could still be asking for something unsafe, such as forwarding an authentication code. Identity alone does not justify every request. Explain the exact action being requested and whether the requester has a legitimate reason to need it. A respectful refusal can coexist with a genuine wish to help. Independent confirmation protects relationships because it reduces mistakes and prevents impersonators from exploiting trust."
    },
    {
      "position": 3,
      "title": "Protecting an account and recovering safely",
      "content": "A strong account protects both the sign-in and the recovery process. Use a different long password for each important account; a password manager can help generate and retain them. Turn on multifactor authentication where available. Treat unexpected approval prompts as warnings rather than minor interruptions. Recovery email addresses, backup codes and trusted devices also deserve protection because someone who controls recovery may be able to bypass an otherwise strong password. Review logged-in sessions through the service's real settings when you suspect misuse.\n\nWorked example: Bisi reused one password on a shopping account and her email. After the shopping service reports a breach, she changes the affected password and any reused copies, enables stronger email authentication and checks recovery methods. Changing only the shopping password would leave the reused email password exposed.\n\nPractise: Design a recovery checklist for a fictional compromised account. Put steps in an order that limits further exposure, and explain why contacting friends is not a substitute for securing the real account.\n\nAccount security is a chain of decisions. The login password, second factor, recovery method, active sessions and connected applications can all affect the ability to control an account. Strong passwords are important, but if a recovery email is abandoned or an unfamiliar device remains signed in, a change to the password alone may not close every route. Review the legitimate service's security settings when concerned. Avoid assuming that every unusual notification proves account theft: assess sign-in records, recent changes and relevant independent confirmations before describing what happened.\n\nPlan for ordinary disruptions. Phones get lost, people change numbers and internet connectivity can be unreliable. An account recovery plan should specify which email or number you control, whether backup methods are current and how the provider confirms ownership. Avoid keeping recovery codes in a public photograph or a shared chat. Where a password manager is used, ensure that its own recovery method is understood and that the person can access important accounts if the primary device is unavailable. A good plan supports legitimate recovery without giving strangers an easy alternative to authentication.\n\nConsider two fictional students whose phones are lost on the same day. One student has reused their email password across many accounts and never checked which devices remain signed in. The other uses separate passwords, an additional authentication method and a documented recovery route. Both must act after the loss, but the second student can prioritise the relevant sessions and recover access more systematically. This comparison does not promise that any configuration makes an account invulnerable; it shows how reducing shared dependencies can limit the consequences of one incident.\n\nPractice writing an incident response in two columns: actions and evidence. 'Changed my password' is an action. 'Confirmed the old credential can no longer authenticate' is one kind of evidence. 'Reviewed active sessions and removed an unfamiliar session' describes another action with a directly observable result. Finish by identifying which recovery settings still require checking and which support team should be contacted if suspicious activity continues. Never include actual passwords or private recovery codes in a learning exercise.\n\nAfter recovery, consider what evidence would make you confident that the problem is resolved. You may have changed the compromised password, but you should also check recovery contacts, application access and unfamiliar active sessions where the service allows it. If an account shows no suspicious activity, that is reassuring but not a universal guarantee. Learn how to explain this limitation to a younger learner without creating fear. Recovery is an informed process with observable checks, not a ceremony completed by pressing one button. Another useful exercise is to ask how your plan changes when you suspect the recovery mailbox itself has been exposed. You may need help from the account provider to restore control over both services in the correct order. Do not assume recovering one account secures every dependent account automatically."
    },
    {
      "position": 4,
      "title": "Sharing information with purpose",
      "content": "Personal information is not limited to identity documents. Photos, locations, routines, school details and contact lists can reveal patterns when combined. Before publishing or uploading something, consider who genuinely needs it, whether the purpose requires each field, and whether the recipient can redistribute it. Privacy controls are useful but imperfect: screenshots and copied messages can outlive an original post. Ask permission before publishing another person's image or location. You can decline a request for unnecessary data or ask how it will be handled.\n\nWorked example: A study group organiser requests full home addresses when only an online meeting link is needed. Members suggest collecting preferred meeting times instead. The organiser still achieves the purpose while reducing data collection and possible harm from an accidental leak.\n\nPractise: Review a fictional event registration form. Mark fields as necessary, optional or unjustified and explain your decision to the organiser without assuming the organiser has malicious intentions.\n\nPrivacy should be connected to a real purpose. A classroom survey about preferred study hours may need a name or contact method to organise groups, but a home address may add little value. A social-media photograph can be enjoyable and meaningful while still exposing personal details such as a badge number, exact location or a friend's identity. Ask who needs the information, how it will be used and how long it should remain accessible. A small group is not a guarantee of secrecy because members may copy or forward its content.\n\nConsent is not a one-time permission covering every use. Someone who agreed to appear in a photograph might not agree to have the image shared on a public page or tagged with their location. Respecting that distinction builds trust and reduces unnecessary harm. When information is about someone else, the question is not only whether you are allowed to access it but also whether you are entitled to distribute it. If an image contains a certificate number or identification detail, crop or conceal it before posting, even if the achievement itself is public.\n\nTry a fictional event-registration exercise. The organiser wants to invite students to a free online workshop and asks for full name, government identity number, home address, school, phone number and preferred session. Separate the truly necessary fields from optional information. Explain how a contact method and session choice might be enough, and whether a display name could meet the need for participation. Consider what the organiser would do with the information after the event. Deleting records that are no longer required can reduce the consequences of a later mistake.\n\nFinally, consider how to respond to an accidental disclosure. If a classmate posts an image containing another student's private information, do not repost it to prove the mistake happened. Notify the affected person and appropriate moderator through a trusted channel and request removal. You cannot guarantee all copies disappear, but you can reduce further spread. Reflection: which matters more when deciding whether to share information, the fact that you possess it or the legitimate purpose of sharing it? Explain using the workshop example.\n\nAn additional consideration is retention. Information gathered for an event may remain on a device long after the organiser needs it. The person who collects data should decide when it can be deleted safely and explain the plan to participants. This also applies to screenshots copied into group chats. A student who helps moderate a club can recommend fewer required fields, limited access to responses and a clear deletion date. Privacy improves when people consider the entire life of the information, not just the moment it is first shared. Think about who could correct inaccurate personal information in an event database. An organiser should offer an appropriate contact for corrections and avoid publishing records more widely than necessary. A clear retention plan also helps decide which old copies can be removed."
    },
    {
      "position": 5,
      "title": "Evaluating claims and online pressure",
      "content": "Misleading content can be false, out of date or missing context. A true photograph paired with a false caption can mislead as effectively as an invented story. Check the original author, publication date, surrounding context and whether trustworthy independent sources support the claim. Pressure phrases such as 'share before they delete it' are not proof. Be particularly careful when a claim asks you to act quickly, expose private information or accuse another person. When you cannot verify a claim, it is reasonable to say so instead of spreading it.\n\nWorked example: A class group circulates a notice announcing an examination date change. The image is a genuine notice from a previous year. Amaka checks the school calendar and the date of the original notice before correcting the group. Forwarding first would have created unnecessary confusion.\n\nPractise: Write an evidence checklist for an urgent social-media claim. Identify at least one reason that repeated sharing cannot substitute for independent corroboration.\n\nAn online claim can be wrong without being entirely invented. A photograph from an earlier year, a true quote without its surrounding context or a genuine notice intended for another group can all create a misleading impression. The important skills are tracing the original source, checking the date and identifying the exact claim being made. A post with many shares may still depend on one uncertain source. When a claim could influence travel, money or another person's reputation, use stronger independent evidence before acting or forwarding it.\n\nExplore the difference between observation and explanation. A photograph may show people waiting outside an office, but does not itself explain why they are there or whether the office has closed. A screenshot may display a warning, but not show which account or service it belongs to. In a lesson exercise, describe only what you can see, then separately list interpretations that require more information. This prevents the temptation to treat the most dramatic caption as the only possible account. Even a genuine image may be attached to an unrelated event.\n\nWorked comparison. A class group circulates two notices: one from an official school website showing the current timetable, and another as a cropped screenshot with no date but many excited comments. A student checks the original issue date of the screenshot and finds that it describes last year's schedule. The student shares the current official notice and explains the mismatch without accusing classmates of knowingly spreading falsehoods. The goal is correcting a decision-relevant fact and preserving trust, not embarrassing people for making an understandable mistake.\n\nPractice creating a verification record. Choose a fictional claim, note where it was first encountered, record its claimed author and date, identify a reliable independent source, and write what remains uncertain. Compare two possible conclusions: 'the message is false' and 'I have not yet confirmed this message'. The second may be more accurate when the evidence is incomplete. Reflection: which online claims deserve an immediate check before sharing, and which harmless opinions do not require the same investigation?\n\nBe careful when an online platform rewards fast replies or repeated sharing. The incentive to be first may conflict with the time needed to establish context. A useful group rule is to share verified source information with the claim, rather than forwarding screenshots that omit dates. If correction is needed, acknowledge the earlier mistake and explain the new evidence. This makes the group more reliable without discouraging useful discussion. Reflection should focus on whether another reader could follow your reasoning and reach the same conclusion. As a final application, write an accurate one-paragraph update about a fictional old warning shared as breaking news. Distinguish the original event, the incorrect present-day claim and the current verified information. Keep the tone helpful and avoid amplifying personal details in the image."
    },
    {
      "position": 6,
      "title": "Device permissions, updates and trusted software",
      "content": "Apps and devices need ongoing care, but not every warning deserves immediate obedience. Install updates through the operating system or a verified publisher route, not through a pop-up that demands protection be disabled. Review app permissions and ask whether a flashlight, calculator or game really needs contacts or precise location. Removing unneeded permissions reduces the information available to a compromised or poorly designed app. Screen locks and backups address different risks: a lock limits casual access, whereas a tested backup helps restore valuable files after loss or damage.\n\nWorked example: Sani sees a message claiming a browser update will fail unless he downloads an unknown installer. He closes the page, opens the browser's own update settings and confirms his installation is already current. The safe step avoids giving the unfamiliar site control over the update process.\n\nPractise: Design a simple monthly device-check routine covering updates, permissions, sign-in protection and one test of whether important files can be recovered.\n\nAn operating system update and a pop-up advertisement are not the same thing. Use the system's own update settings or a verified publisher channel to obtain software, rather than clicking an unexpected page that insists a device is infected. This reduces exposure to impostor installers and misleading prompts. Updates correct known defects, but devices also need permissions, screen locks and backups appropriate to the owner's situation. Security is rarely a single button or tool. Different safeguards protect against different failure scenarios.\n\nConsider a device used for schoolwork. Its owner may need document storage, occasional camera access and reliable internet. A simple game requesting continuous location, microphone and contacts should prompt questions about relevance. Some features require these capabilities; others do not. Review the app's explanation and choose a narrower permission when available. If a feature no longer works, decide whether the convenience justifies the access rather than assuming the permission is automatically harmful or automatically necessary.\n\nWorked scenario: a learner accidentally deletes a folder containing a term's assignments. The laptop had received security updates and used a screen lock, but neither safeguard restores deleted files. A separate backup that was actually tested may help recover the assignments. This demonstrates why availability and recovery deserve attention alongside access protection. A backup is more reassuring when the owner knows what was saved, when it was saved and whether the needed files can be restored without destroying the current work.\n\nPractice designing a device care schedule for a household with limited bandwidth. Specify a realistic time to check updates, review newly granted permissions and test recovery of one important document. The plan should accommodate connectivity costs and shared-device rules. Record a specific result for each check, not just a tick mark. Reflection: why is keeping a device updated different from confirming that important work could be recovered after its loss? A good answer distinguishes prevention, access control and recovery.\n\nAccessibility can also change a sensible device routine. Some learners use assistive tools, older phones or shared public computers. An update process or security setting that blocks essential accessibility software should be investigated rather than ignored. A safer plan identifies the function, the compatibility issue and an appropriate support route. It should never instruct a learner to disable all device protections permanently as a shortcut. For a shared computer, remember to sign out of sensitive sessions and avoid saving account secrets in public browser profiles. If an important update cannot be installed immediately because of limited data, document the limitation and plan the next appropriate opportunity. Do not download an unknown alternative installer merely because it promises a faster fix. Check through the legitimate platform. Verify carefully."
    },
    {
      "position": 7,
      "title": "Responding when something goes wrong",
      "content": "The correct response depends on what actually happened. Receiving a suspicious message is different from clicking a link, downloading a file or entering credentials. Describe the event accurately before taking action. If you gave away a password, use the legitimate service's recovery process and change reused passwords. If a device behaves unexpectedly, disconnect it from risky services where appropriate and seek trusted technical assistance rather than installing random 'repair' tools. Report suspected abuse through the platform, organisation or responsible adult without circulating the harmful content. Keep a brief timeline of what you observed and what you changed.\n\nWorked example: Ife receives an unexpected login alert after reusing a password. She checks activity through the authentic app, changes the password, revokes unfamiliar sessions and reviews recovery settings. She records the event and alerts the relevant support team. She does not claim a complete investigation when she only has an alert.\n\nPractise: Create an incident diary for a fictional lost phone. Record when it was last seen, which accounts matter most, whom to notify and how you would confirm that recovery steps worked.\n\nWhen something unexpected happens, distinguish the event from your interpretation. A suspicious message does not prove a password was exposed. An unfamiliar account session may warrant investigation but does not establish how it was created. A device error can result from misconfiguration as well as misuse. Write down the time, observed message, affected service and what you actually did before taking further steps. This short record helps support staff respond without guessing or causing unnecessary alarm.\n\nOrder actions according to the harm they address. If you entered credentials through an unfamiliar page, use the genuine service's recovery process and review reused passwords and sessions. If you downloaded an unknown installer, seek trusted technical help and avoid opening more files or attempting random repairs. If a threat involves payment information, contact the relevant provider using established details. Do not forward suspicious links to friends as a test. The correct path depends on the service, evidence and consequence.\n\nWorked case: two learners receive the same fraudulent account message. One merely reads it and deletes it; the other enters a reused password. The first may need to report the attempted deception and remain vigilant. The second has an exposure that justifies timely account recovery, password changes and session review. Treating both situations as proven account compromise would obscure the difference. Good incident response avoids both complacency and unsupported claims that everyone has been hacked.\n\nCreate an incident diary using fictional details. Record observations, decisions, actions and results in separate columns. Explain which setting changes protect the account and which observations provide evidence that recovery succeeded. If an action has a side effect, such as signing out every device, note how that affects legitimate users. Reflection: why is a calm, accurate report more useful than a dramatic claim about an attacker? The answer should discuss evidence quality, timely support and preventing further disclosure of private information.\n\nConsider how information about an incident should be communicated to others. If a school account was misused, notifying an administrator may be necessary, but posting a complete activity log in a public class group can expose other people. Provide only the relevant facts to the responsible contact using an appropriate channel. Maintain a record of the support request and any actions completed. The goal is to enable useful help while reducing the chance that the response itself creates a second privacy problem. When no trusted helper is immediately reachable, avoid taking further risky actions solely to resolve uncertainty. Preserve a concise note of the event and return to the official support process when available. Uncertainty is a reason to be careful, not to improvise credential sharing."
    },
    {
      "position": 8,
      "title": "Putting safe choices into a personal plan",
      "content": "Digital safety improves when decisions become ordinary habits rather than emergency reactions. Select a few repeatable safeguards: independent verification, unique account credentials, careful sharing, trusted updates and a clear reporting route. A plan should be realistic for the devices and access you have. If connectivity is limited, keep trusted contact information offline and plan how to recover accounts without assuming a second device is always available. Include other people respectfully: helping a friend means offering guidance, not collecting their password or taking over their account.\n\nWorked example: A family creates a shared rule for unexpected money requests. They verify through a known contact, agree that urgency does not remove the check, and show younger relatives how to report suspicious messages. Each person retains control of their own accounts.\n\nReflection: Explain three choices you would make differently after this course, which evidence would support each choice, and one case where you would ask for help.\n\nFurther reading (independent resources; links should be checked before production publication):\nCISA Secure Our World — https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world\nNIST Cybersecurity Framework — https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework\nNational Cyber Security Centre guidance — https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/collection/top-tips-for-staying-secure-online\n\nA personal security plan should cover repeatable ordinary choices rather than depend on constant fear. Choose a few specific habits that reduce common risks: independent verification, distinct account passwords, careful sharing, verified software updates and a reliable recovery route. Each action should be possible with the devices and connectivity you actually have. A plan that assumes expensive software or a permanent internet connection may fail its intended users. Good design makes the safer choice easier to remember under pressure.\n\nShared devices raise practical questions. A family or study group may need to agree on separate user accounts, sign-out practices and how to report a lost device. The agreement should protect each person's autonomy. Helping someone with an account does not mean collecting their password or forcing them to approve a sign-in you initiated. Where a learner is young or needs assistance, trusted adults can explain risks and help use legitimate support channels while respecting dignity and privacy.\n\nWorked scenario: after several messages impersonating family members, a household agrees to confirm unexpected money requests through a previously established contact. They also review important recovery details and test whether one valued photograph can be restored from backup. The plan combines prevention, verification and recovery without claiming that any one step eliminates all risk. When a new scam circulates, the household shares the verification approach rather than forwarding suspicious links around the family.\n\nPractice constructing a one-page digital safety charter. Include the habit, why it matters, how to check it and what to do when it fails. Ask another learner to read the plan and point out unclear language. A strong document should make sense even when the writer is not present to explain it. Reflection: which two habits would you keep if you could only maintain a very short routine, and what important risks would remain? Identify a responsible route for asking for help.\n\nRevisit the charter after a life change, such as switching schools, changing devices or beginning a job. The most important accounts and trusted contacts may change, and a recovery plan can become outdated silently. Make one improvement at a time and verify it. Good security learning is not the memorisation of a fixed checklist; it is the ability to identify what matters, select reasonable protective actions and explain the evidence behind them. This is the foundation for more advanced technical and professional learning. A good charter can also teach others without shaming them. Explain one safe choice in simple language, invite questions and allow room for different devices or circumstances. The objective is practical confidence, not a perfect score on a checklist."
    }
  ]
}
